        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Phoenicians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The coast of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, chiefly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> with neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Israel; settlements on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1200–332 BCE, from the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> kingdoms to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>’s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>; in the west, Punic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> carried the culture on until 146 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The alphabet; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a> and the cedar trade; seafaring and colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Gadir (Cádiz); carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>, decorated metal bowls and glass</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, Sarepta, Kition, Motya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>; inscriptions such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a>, Tabnit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Assyrian royal annals, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a></span></div>
        </div>
