    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Phoenicians were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> heirs of the Canaanite towns of the Lebanese coast: not one nation but rival city-states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Arwad, each with its own king.</li><li>Their merchants sold cedar, purple cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and metalwork across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, and from the 9th century BCE founded settlements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</li><li>Their alphabet of 22 letters, taken up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a>, is the ancestor of the Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic scripts.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how Phoenician sailors sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a> sailed round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in three years. He doubted their report that the sun had been on their right, the very detail that leads many scholars to take the voyage seriously.</p></aside>
    </section>

