    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200–1150 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> kingdoms collapse; the cities of the Lebanese coast emerge as independent kingdoms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The sarcophagus of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> is inscribed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">10th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a> makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> the leading city; by the biblical account he sends cedar for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>’s temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">814 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Tyrians traditionally found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, as settlements spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">677 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> captures rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and deports its people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 585–572 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> withstands a thirteen-year siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The cities pass under Persian rule; their ships form the core of the Persian navy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">332 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> after a siege of about seven months</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun.jpg" alt="Story of Wenamun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenamun-papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ancient Egyptian Writer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Phoenicians were the people of the coastal cities of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, a narrow strip of land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, roughly present-day Lebanon with neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Israel. They spoke Phoenician, a Semitic language closely related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. “Phoenicians” is the name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> gave them, already in the poems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>; it is probably connected with phoinix, a Greek word for a purple-red colour, though its origin is debated. They never formed a single state, and their inscriptions name people by their cities, as Tyrians, Sidonians or men of Gebal, the city the Greeks called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> often calls them all Sidonians.</p><p>They were not newcomers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> recorded a tradition that they had come from the Erythraean Sea, the Greek name for the waters around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, but archaeologists see no sign of such a migration: the language, gods and crafts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> cities continue those of the Canaanite towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Byblos had shipped cedar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> since the 3rd millennium BCE, and further north the Kingdom of Ugarit, which wrote an alphabet of its own in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> signs, flourished as a great port until its destruction c. 1185 BCE.</p><p>The Phoenician age is usually counted from the crisis of about 1200–1150 BCE, when the palace kingdoms of the eastern Mediterranean collapsed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> fell, Ugarit and the Mycenaean palaces were destroyed, and Egypt lost its provinces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. The cities of the Lebanese coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, Byblos, Arwad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> among them, came through comparatively unharmed and, freed from Egyptian and Hittite overlords, emerged as independent kingdoms. Sarepta, between Sidon and Tyre, excavated by James B. Pritchard from 1969 to 1974, is still the most fully explored town of the homeland, its layers running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> into the Iron Age.</p><p>The Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a>, set about 1075 BCE, shows the new balance of power. Its hero, an official of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> sent to buy cedar for the god’s sacred barque, is kept waiting for weeks in the harbour of Byblos, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakar-baal">Zakar-Baal</a> makes him pay for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> like any other customer, producing his forefathers’ accounts to show that Egypt had always paid. By the 10th century BCE Tyre, built on an island a short distance offshore, had become the leading city, and most of the later colonies of the west would come from it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii.jpg" alt="Eshmunazar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmunazar_II_sarcophagus_(31289612892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred Romero from Paris, France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There was never a Phoenician kingdom. Each city ruled a small territory on the coast and in the hills behind it, with its own gods and its own king, and the cities competed as often as they cooperated: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> led from the 10th to the 6th century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> under Persian rule. Kings governed with councils of elders and the heads of great merchant families; the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> called Tyre the city “whose merchants are princes”. At Sidon the royal family also served the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> as priests, as the inscribed sarcophagi of King Tabnit and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a> record.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a>, king of Tyre in the 10th century BCE, appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as the ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, sending cedar, cypress and craftsmen for the temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and joining him in voyages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophir">Ophir</a>. The Tyrian annals that the Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> quoted from Menander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> say that he enlarged the island city and rebuilt its temples. In the 9th century BCE Ithobaal I of Tyre, whom the Books of Kings call Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians, married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, king of Israel, an alliance the biblical writers remembered with hostility.</p><p>The homeland had little farmland, and its wealth came from the sea, the forests and the workshops. The mountains supplied cedar and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> prized in treeless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Dyers extracted purple from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">murex</a> sea snails in such numbers that a great mound of crushed shells survives by the southern harbour of Sidon, and cloth coloured with this Tyrian purple was a luxury of kings. Craftsmen worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, bronze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold and glass, and merchants dealt in wine, oil, textiles, metals and slaves. The prophet Ezekiel’s lament for Tyre, from the early 6th century BCE, pictures the city as a splendid merchant ship and names its trading partners from Tarshish in the far west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and Mesopotamia.</p><p>Daily life is known mostly from tombs and small excavations, because the ancient cities lie beneath modern towns. Houses of stone and mudbrick stood close together inside the walls, and people lived on bread, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and fish. Many men were sailors, shipwrights or craftsmen, and some lived for long periods abroad, as in the quarter of Memphis in Egypt known, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says, as the Camp of the Tyrians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmun.jpg" alt="Eshmun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmun_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BlingBling10 at en.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Phoenician religion is known from inscriptions, temples, images and burials, from the hostile accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and of Greek and Roman writers, and from the Phoenician History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a>, a Greek work of the Roman period that claimed to translate the ancient writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchuniathon">Sanchuniathon</a> and survives only in quotations by Eusebius. Texts from Ugarit, written centuries earlier, show that many of its gods and myths were an old Canaanite inheritance.</p><p>Each city had its own chief gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a>, “the Lady of Byblos”, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a>, a god of healing, whose great sanctuary near the Awali river outside the city was rebuilt on a grand scale by its kings in the Persian period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a>, “king of the city”, whose “awakening” was celebrated each year at a festival that, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a> was the first to hold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, goddess of love, fertility and war, was widely worshipped, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, “lord”, was a title borne by several gods, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalshamin">Baalshamin</a>, “lord of heaven”. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> matched these gods with their own: Melqart with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, Astarte with Aphrodite, Eshmun with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> visited the temple of Melqart at Tyre in the 5th century BCE and saw two pillars, one of gold and the other, as he understood it, of emerald, which shone at night; the priests told him that the temple was as old as the city, 2,300 years.</p><p>Worship took place in temples and in open-air sanctuaries with altars and standing stones, where priests offered animals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, food and drink. The dead were buried in rock-cut tombs or cremated, and kings were laid in stone sarcophagi inscribed with curses on anyone who disturbed them, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> at Byblos and Tabnit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a> at Sidon.</p><p>The most disputed question is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Bible condemns the burning of children, a practice it attributes to the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, and Greek and Roman writers accused the Phoenicians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> of sacrificing children in times of danger. Sanctuaries called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tophet">tophets</a>, with urns holding the burnt bones of infants and young animals, have been excavated at Carthage and at Phoenician and Punic settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, but none has been securely identified in the homeland. Scholars disagree over whether the tophets record sacrifice or were cemeteries for infants who died of natural causes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahiram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahiram.jpg" alt="Ahiram" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahiram_sarcophag_from_Biblos_XIII-XBC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matson Photo Service, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Phoenicians’ most lasting creation was their alphabet. In the 2nd millennium BCE people in Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> had begun to write with signs that each stood for a single consonant; in the Phoenician cities these settled into a fixed set of 22 letters, written from right to left, which appears fully formed in the epitaph that Ittobaal, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, carved on the sarcophagus of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> around 1000 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a> adopted it, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> square script, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Arabic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> borrowed it in the 9th or 8th century BCE and used some of its letters for vowels, and through Greek and Etruscan it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says that the Greeks called their letters phoinikeia, “Phoenician letters”, brought by the companions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>.</p><p>Little Phoenician literature survives. Books on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and parchment have perished, among them the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> quoted, leaving inscriptions on stone and metal, most of them short dedications and epitaphs; the royal texts of Byblos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and the bilingual inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatepe">Karatepe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> are the chief exceptions.</p><p>Phoenician craftsmen were famous across the ancient world. Carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> panels for furniture in Phoenician styles have been found in the Assyrian palaces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, where the Bible speaks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>’s “ivory house”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> and bronze bowls engraved with Egyptian-style scenes travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and princely tombs in Italy. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> the prize for the footrace at the funeral games of Patroclus is a silver mixing bowl made by Sidonian craftsmen and carried over the sea by Phoenician traders. Workshops also produced glass vessels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, jewellery, seals and amulets, freely combining Egyptian, Syrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> motifs.</p><p>The Phoenicians were the most skilled shipbuilders and navigators of their age. Assyrian palace reliefs show their round-hulled merchant ships and oared warships, and Greek writers said that while Greek sailors steered by the Great Bear, the Phoenicians steered by the Little Bear, which turns closer to the pole and which the Greeks called Phoinike. Herodotus reports that Phoenician crews sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a>, who reigned from 610 to 595 BCE, sailed south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, put ashore each autumn to sow and reap a crop, and returned through the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in the third year; whether the voyage really took place is still debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tharros"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tharros.jpg" alt="Tharros" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tharros">Tharros</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:At_Sardinia_2024_045.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 10th century BCE Phoenician goods and traders reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and in the 9th and 8th centuries BCE Phoenicians founded settlements across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, most of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. At Kition on Cyprus, Phoenician settlers were rebuilding the town’s sanctuaries by about 850 BCE. Further west they settled at Utica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, at Motya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Panormus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solunto">Soloeis</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, at Nora, Sulcis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharros">Tharros</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and along the coasts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where Gadir, modern Cádiz, beyond the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, grew rich on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of Tartessos. Ancient writers dated Gadir and Utica to about 1100 BCE and Carthage to 814 BCE, but archaeological finds point to the 9th and 8th centuries BCE. The search for metals, trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, a shortage of land at home and Assyrian demands for tribute all seem to have driven the expansion, though scholars weigh them differently. Carthage grew into a power in its own right, the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>, which dominated the western Mediterranean until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed the city in 146 BCE.</p><p>With the Greeks the Phoenicians traded and competed. Greek art of the 8th and 7th centuries BCE borrowed many eastern motifs that Phoenician goods carried west, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> wrote that when Greek colonists came to Sicily in large numbers, the Phoenicians drew back to the west of the island, around Motya, Soloeis and Panormus.</p><p>The great land empires pressed on the cities throughout their history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> of Assyria took tribute from Tyre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Arwad about 870 BCE, and the bronze gates of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> at Balawat show Tyrian tribute ferried from the island. In 701 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>’s advance drove the king of Sidon to flee overseas, and in 677 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> captured rebellious Sidon and deported its people; its king, Abdi-Milkutti, escaped by sea but was caught and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> besieged Tyre for thirteen years from about 585 BCE until the city came to terms. In 539 BCE the cities passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, which they served for two centuries: their ships formed the core of the Persian fleet against the Greeks, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> rated the Sidonian ships the best in the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, defeated at Salamis in 480 BCE. He also says that when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> ordered an attack on Carthage, the Phoenicians refused to make war on their own children, and the plan was dropped.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tennes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tennes.jpg" alt="Tennes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tennes">Tennes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PHOENICIA,_Sidon._Tennes._Circa_351-347_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Phoenician independence ended in the 4th century BCE. Around 351 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, under its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennes">Tennes</a>, led a revolt against the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a>; when the Persian army approached, Tennes betrayed the city, and the Sidonians, Diodorus says, set fire to it rather than surrender. After defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> in 333 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> marched down the coast. Arwad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Sidon opened their gates, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> would not let him sacrifice in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> within its walls. Alexander besieged the island city for about seven months, building a causeway to it from the mainland, and took it by storm in the summer of 332 BCE. Thousands of its defenders were killed, and by the ancient accounts between 13,000 and 30,000 of its people were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The causeway, widened over the centuries by sand, still joins the old island to the shore.</p><p>Under Alexander’s successors and, from 64 BCE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the cities remained busy ports, but Greek gradually replaced Phoenician in public life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, came from Kition on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinus-of-tyre">Marinus of Tyre</a> and the philosopher Porphyry, also of Tyre, wrote in Greek under Roman rule. In the west the Phoenician language lived on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and its world, and Punic was still spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a>, around 400 CE.</p><p>Modern study began with the decipherment of the script by Jean-Jacques Barthélemy in 1758, from a bilingual dedication to Melqart found on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>’s Mission de Phénicie of 1860–1861. The excavations of Osman Hamdi Bey in the royal necropolis of Sidon in 1887 recovered the sarcophagus of King Tabnit and the Alexander Sarcophagus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram-sarcophagus">sarcophagus of Ahiram</a> at Byblos in 1923. Byblos and Tyre are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, but much of the ancient cities lies under modern towns.</p><p>Views of the Phoenicians have changed. Greek and Roman writers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s tales of Phoenician kidnappers to Roman writing against Carthage, cast them as cunning traders and servants of cruel gods, an image repeated for centuries. Modern historians stress instead their part in carrying goods, techniques and ideas between East and West. Some, notably Josephine Quinn, question whether the people of Tyre, Sidon and Byblos ever thought of themselves as one people rather than as citizens of their cities. In the 20th century some Lebanese writers claimed the Phoenicians as the ancestors of a distinct Lebanese identity, an idea that remains debated.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Phoenicians, the seafaring people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and the other cities of the Lebanese coast, flourished from about 1200 BCE until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took Tyre in 332 BCE. Their merchants linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> with the far west, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Gadir, and their alphabet, taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a>, became the ancestor of the Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic scripts.</em></p>

